# Benefits

The architecture provides:

## Separation of Concerns

Each layer has clearly defined responsibilities.

---

## Testability

Business logic can be tested without:

- Firestore
- Qdrant
- Gemini
- OpenSearch
- Cloud infrastructure

Infrastructure dependencies can be mocked.

---

## Maintainability

Business logic remains isolated from implementation details.

Infrastructure changes rarely affect domain logic.

---

## Flexibility

Technology can be replaced with minimal impact.

Examples include replacing:

- Gemini → Claude
- Firestore → PostgreSQL
- Qdrant → Pinecone
- OpenSearch → Elasticsearch

without changing business rules.

---

## Scalability

Individual infrastructure components can evolve independently.

Examples

- New retrieval engines
- New AI models
- Additional MCP servers
- Alternative storage providers

---

# Alternatives Considered

## Traditional Layered Architecture

```text
Controller

↓

Service

↓

Repository

↓

Database
```

Advantages

- Simple
- Familiar
- Easy to understand

Disadvantages

- High coupling
- Business logic leaks into controllers
- Difficult technology replacement

Decision

Not selected.

---

## Monolithic Service Architecture

Advantages

- Rapid initial development

Disadvantages

- Poor maintainability
- Limited scalability
- Difficult testing

Decision

Not selected.

---

## Hexagonal Architecture

Advantages

- Excellent isolation
- Strong abstraction

Disadvantages

- Additional complexity
- More interfaces

Decision

Clean Architecture is adopted while applying Ports and Adapters principles where appropriate.

---

# Consequences

## Positive

- Clear architectural boundaries
- Easier testing
- Better maintainability
- Framework independence
- Cloud independence
- AI model independence
- Easier onboarding of developers
- Better long-term evolution

---

## Negative

- More project structure
- Additional abstraction
- More interfaces
- Slightly steeper learning curve

---

# Risks

Potential risks include:

- Excessive abstraction
- Over-engineering
- Interface proliferation
- Increased development effort for simple features

These risks should be managed by applying abstraction only where it provides long-term value.

---

# Architecture Impact

This decision affects every major component of the platform, including:

- API design
- Dependency injection
- Service implementation
- Repository design
- AI integration
- Tool integration
- MCP architecture
- Testing strategy
- Deployment

All new features should conform to the established architectural boundaries.

---

# Review

This decision should be reviewed if:

- Platform requirements fundamentally change
- Architectural complexity outweighs benefits
- A different architectural style better supports future business needs

